1
00:00:16,345 --> 00:00:29,190
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the Anguttara Nikaya, I think it's Bhikkhuni Gautami, she's asking the Buddha, how do I know if something is dhamma or not dhamma?

2
00:00:30,972 --> 00:00:44,018
[SPEAKER_00]: And he gives us those eight ways that you can figure out if this thing that is being taught or this thing that you are understanding is, in fact, the dhamma.

3
00:00:44,038 --> 00:00:45,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Which things did he give us?

4
00:00:48,047 --> 00:00:53,112
[SPEAKER_00]: This leads to dispassion, doesn't lead to passion, and so on.

5
00:00:53,532 --> 00:01:04,124
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just taking passion, for example, the dhamma leads to non-anger, not anger, to passion, to dispassion, not to passion.

6
00:01:06,059 --> 00:01:10,061
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I was saying in the past, when we spoke about it.

7
00:01:10,401 --> 00:01:17,883
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's not practical, in a sense of, if it's not freeing you from suffering, if it's not freeing you from greed, aversion, delusion, it's not a dhamma.

8
00:01:19,124 --> 00:01:20,064
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the criteria.

9
00:01:20,944 --> 00:01:33,849
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if there is no practical application of it, that has a direct result in dispassion, disenchantment, it's not a dhamma, even if it's the dhamma you're reading and talking about it in the suttas and nothing else.

10
00:01:36,492 --> 00:01:49,996
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the difference is... the difference would be determined then by the way you took it on that individual level, personally.

11
00:01:50,876 --> 00:01:58,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can have people dedicate their whole life to dhamma, study of the dhamma, even understand the dhamma, so to speak, but it's all on the level of ideas.

12
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:06,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, for somebody, is the way to kind of enter the proper practical application of dhamma.

13
00:02:07,545 --> 00:02:10,067
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not necessarily guaranteed.

14
00:02:11,048 --> 00:02:13,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it can happen that you just stay on the level of ideas.

15
00:02:14,731 --> 00:02:16,632
[SPEAKER_01]: And then things can make sense, absolutely.

16
00:02:17,373 --> 00:02:21,696
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can connect and find connections in your ideas, in all the suttas.

17
00:02:22,396 --> 00:02:25,379
[SPEAKER_01]: But are these ideas, when they're present in my mind,

18
00:02:26,273 --> 00:02:34,474
[SPEAKER_01]: When they endure in my mind as ideas, are they resulting in me being unable to be compassionate while those ideas are there?

19
00:02:35,115 --> 00:02:38,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Unable to be filled with ill-will while those ideas are there?

20
00:02:38,955 --> 00:02:42,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Unable to be distracted while those ideas are there?

21
00:02:44,196 --> 00:02:47,657
[SPEAKER_01]: And if the answer to any of this is no, it's not the dhamma then.

22
00:02:49,137 --> 00:02:52,498
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's the difference between like knowing about the dhamma and knowing the dhamma.

23
00:02:53,318 --> 00:02:54,378
[SPEAKER_01]: You can have an absolute

24
00:02:55,448 --> 00:03:03,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Fully familiar, expert Pali scholar, fluent in Pali and everything, and still be a patujana, not free from any of the factors at all.

25
00:03:05,173 --> 00:03:09,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Know all the suttas by heart.

26
00:03:10,676 --> 00:03:14,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, so, that's what I mean, but you need some knowledge.

27
00:03:14,998 --> 00:03:20,381
[SPEAKER_01]: But knowledge starts on the level of perceiving information and collecting information.

28
00:03:20,561 --> 00:03:21,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not knowledge in itself.

29
00:03:24,195 --> 00:03:32,682
[SPEAKER_01]: That which you perceive, that information that you gather, that you remember now, will become knowledge if you start applying it, and if it starts having that practical effect.

30
00:03:34,803 --> 00:03:46,212
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you, you know, if you think like, oh, I spend lots of time thinking about dhamma, yeah, that's fine, but now you want to see, are those thoughts, while I'm thinking about dhamma, are they resulting in dispassion, while they're there?

31
00:03:47,413 --> 00:03:49,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, am I distracted on account of those thoughts?

32
00:03:50,415 --> 00:03:52,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I distracted on account of my sutta studies?

33
00:03:54,400 --> 00:04:00,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I get agitated on account of me exploring these different views and so on and trying to understand the suttas?

34
00:04:01,266 --> 00:04:03,988
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, means you're not taking it on that practical level.

35
00:04:05,029 --> 00:04:05,729
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not the dhamma.

36
00:04:07,551 --> 00:04:09,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is fine if you recognize it.

37
00:04:09,592 --> 00:04:10,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can sort of start.

38
00:04:11,073 --> 00:04:14,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so let me learn how to think without passion, without greed, without aversion.

39
00:04:15,157 --> 00:04:17,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, whatever I think will be the dhamma.

40
00:04:17,518 --> 00:04:19,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what determines whether it's the dhamma or not.

41
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:22,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Not whether you're thinking and reciting that which you read in the suttas.

42
00:04:24,016 --> 00:04:25,117
[SPEAKER_01]: And repeating other words.

43
00:04:25,197 --> 00:04:26,718
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not what makes dhamma dhamma.

44
00:04:29,099 --> 00:04:40,607
[SPEAKER_01]: It's whether those phenomena, when they're present in your experience, in your mind, are preventing you from experiencing greed, aversion, delusion.

45
00:04:40,707 --> 00:04:45,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Are they making a reason greed, aversion, delusion decrease, and non-reason not arise?

46
00:04:46,090 --> 00:04:47,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Or they don't make any difference to that?

47
00:04:48,632 --> 00:04:51,694
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when it comes down to... it's on the level of the view.

48
00:04:51,814 --> 00:04:52,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Because many people would

49
00:04:54,371 --> 00:05:13,164
[SPEAKER_01]: People would believe that, you know, I'm thinking about the dhamma, I'm practicing the dhamma, but they would not necessarily see, are those very thoughts, are they serving as a distraction, on the level of conviction, on the level of certain sort of inspiration only?

50
00:05:13,724 --> 00:05:17,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are those thoughts really, while they're there, making me dispassionate?

51
00:05:18,367 --> 00:05:21,730
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, even when they're not there, I'm dispassionate on account of those thoughts that I had.

52
00:05:22,578 --> 00:05:27,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Or there is no such an effect, or you can't see it, or you're still doubting it.

53
00:05:28,462 --> 00:05:30,904
[SPEAKER_01]: If any of this is the case, means it's not the dhamma.

54
00:05:32,045 --> 00:05:33,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if the dhamma is all you think about.

55
00:05:35,328 --> 00:05:45,095
[SPEAKER_00]: And one way you can see whether you know what you're talking about, if what you're talking about is the dhamma, is through discussion.

56
00:05:46,143 --> 00:05:50,485
[SPEAKER_00]: With others, who have an opposing view, because...

57
00:05:50,926 --> 00:05:57,069
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example, like internet forums, or one-on-one discussion, you know.

58
00:05:58,250 --> 00:06:07,755
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you are speaking about the dhamma, and someone criticizes that view, and you get angry,

59
00:06:08,715 --> 00:06:13,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but it's still fully convicted, the conviction that you know the dhamma.

60
00:06:13,699 --> 00:06:17,562
[SPEAKER_00]: That should be the indicator that that is not what... that's not the dhamma yet.

61
00:06:17,582 --> 00:06:18,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's not the dhamma.

62
00:06:18,803 --> 00:06:28,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if the view that you've been talking about, it's all based on the suttas and so on, you are not seeing it on the level of dispassion, which means it's not the dhamma for you.

63
00:06:30,312 --> 00:06:31,333
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't even need

64
00:06:32,168 --> 00:06:50,303
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, that that can be helpful, but even if you don't actually talk to others or engage in for them, you can just see it on your individual level, whereby engage in some reflection, a kind of some sort of trying to understand, and then see afterwards, when you sort of get tired of it and so on, are you calmer?

65
00:06:50,523 --> 00:06:52,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are you just distracted and tired?

66
00:06:53,225 --> 00:06:57,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are you brighter and sharper and full of energy as a result of it?

67
00:06:57,529 --> 00:06:58,450
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you think?

68
00:06:58,490 --> 00:07:00,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you agitated?

69
00:07:00,291 --> 00:07:01,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are you composed?

70
00:07:03,004 --> 00:07:16,229
[SPEAKER_00]: Any effort into investigating the dhamma or dwelling in dhamma should not, at the end of that, when you... You should not be more liable to passion or your will.

71
00:07:16,689 --> 00:07:19,230
[SPEAKER_00]: What the leftover will be, peace.

72
00:07:19,270 --> 00:07:19,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

73
00:07:20,131 --> 00:07:28,014
[SPEAKER_01]: So you need to then, that's what I mean, like you need to learn then how to think without greed, without aversion, without delusion, whereby your own effort or inspiration will not induce those.

74
00:07:29,503 --> 00:07:30,804
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you cannot get angry.

75
00:07:30,925 --> 00:07:34,568
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot get passionate on account of thinking the things that are the dhamma.

76
00:07:34,588 --> 00:07:37,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, literally, any phenomenon that arises in your mind is the dhamma.

77
00:07:39,173 --> 00:07:41,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's what dhamma is.

78
00:07:41,355 --> 00:07:45,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding of those things, understanding of this passion in regard to those things, that's the dhamma, is the practice.

79
00:07:49,764 --> 00:07:50,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of...

80
00:07:52,114 --> 00:07:53,636
[SPEAKER_00]: What should you do if you want to study?

81
00:07:53,716 --> 00:08:07,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, like, you study and stuff, but as I said, you have to keep an eye on yourself in the process of doing so, and not start sort of... if you feel you're getting fatigued and lapsing, and now you're doing it mechanically, stop.

82
00:08:07,225 --> 00:08:10,487
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to stop and regain the composure, because that's the foremost.

83
00:08:10,547 --> 00:08:12,068
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have too much of the composure.

84
00:08:12,189 --> 00:08:16,812
[SPEAKER_00]: But also, isn't it about application, applying what you are...

85
00:08:17,232 --> 00:08:19,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's... yeah, that's where it's applied.

86
00:08:19,954 --> 00:08:29,181
[SPEAKER_01]: You start applying it by not act... by not basically engaging in reading, in study, in inspiration, that's fueled with craving anymore.

87
00:08:29,201 --> 00:08:33,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Because initially, whatever you do, you crave towards it, so to speak.

88
00:08:33,084 --> 00:08:35,806
[SPEAKER_01]: It's underlined by craving, even your own thinking.

89
00:08:36,747 --> 00:08:38,088
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you're not free from passion.

90
00:08:38,628 --> 00:08:45,233
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you realize, wait, so I can think these things with craving, but these things can also be thought without craving.

91
00:08:47,218 --> 00:08:48,780
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what you start to learn.

92
00:08:49,821 --> 00:08:50,321
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what I mean.

93
00:08:50,341 --> 00:08:53,424
[SPEAKER_01]: You learn how to think without greed, without aversion, without delusion.

94
00:08:54,746 --> 00:09:06,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only time greed, aversion, delusion arises, when you lose perspective, basically when you lapse in terms of your self-awareness, and get focused on the argument in point that you are making, even if it's with the right argument.

95
00:09:07,419 --> 00:09:09,361
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that reason, that's not a dhamma anymore.

96
00:09:12,022 --> 00:09:18,608
[SPEAKER_01]: And that applies to like the broadest sense, to the most particular sort of, you know, Abhidhamma style discussion.

97
00:09:19,709 --> 00:09:23,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you... is this resulting in increasing of passion or not?

98
00:09:24,293 --> 00:09:30,539
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, those ideas are not seen on the level they should be seen, or those ideas are wrong, one way or the other.

99
00:09:30,559 --> 00:09:34,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Dhamma is only found on that personal level, not on the level of forums and others.

100
00:09:36,130 --> 00:09:36,771
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be like that.

101
00:09:36,791 --> 00:09:39,314
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why miracle of instruction that the Buddha would say.

102
00:09:39,334 --> 00:09:46,543
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you teach dhamma to somebody and if they get to see that dhamma that you are seeing for yourself, they get to see for themselves, it's on the level of the miracle.

103
00:09:47,364 --> 00:09:49,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Like flying in the sky and touching the sun and so on.

104
00:09:51,189 --> 00:09:53,052
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said that's the only miracle he approves of.

105
00:09:57,747 --> 00:10:08,373
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you do come across and say on the forum somebody approves, somebody disapproves of your... of your view, if you're getting personally disturbed or defensive or something, means you haven't... you are not clear with your own view.

106
00:10:09,433 --> 00:10:13,636
[SPEAKER_01]: You were presenting it already with a preference, with a bias, with underlying craving.

107
00:10:14,196 --> 00:10:27,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you yourself were completely free from doubt in regard to that view, there would be no room for passion, because there's no room for doubt, no room for real will, because there's no room for doubt, and no room for distraction, because there's no room for doubt.

108
00:10:28,853 --> 00:10:33,417
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if somebody approves of that view, you will not be like, yeah, okay, he sees the same thing.

109
00:10:33,457 --> 00:10:34,758
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be elated on account of it.

110
00:10:35,098 --> 00:10:36,799
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody disapproves, you would just...

111
00:10:36,819 --> 00:10:40,262
[SPEAKER_01]: The only thing that would reveal to yourself is, like, to stop wasting your time with that person.

112
00:10:41,483 --> 00:10:42,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, like, because you're offended.

113
00:10:42,604 --> 00:10:44,666
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems like, oh, it's like, like flogging a dead horse.

114
00:10:46,178 --> 00:10:53,681
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just you try, you try, you get the point across, and they still don't accept, they still disagree with it, they still say you're wrong, fine, no problem.

115
00:10:53,701 --> 00:10:56,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Like as many times happened in the suttas, even to the Buddha himself.

116
00:10:57,222 --> 00:11:03,845
[SPEAKER_01]: When he would say something, then a person would not... didn't like what they hear, so they said, no, no, I don't think that's the case.

117
00:11:04,045 --> 00:11:05,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Like with the children, wasn't it?

118
00:11:05,285 --> 00:11:06,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Children are the suffering, the Buddha says.

119
00:11:07,326 --> 00:11:10,267
[SPEAKER_01]: And that man says, no, no, I think you're wrong.

120
00:11:10,287 --> 00:11:11,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Children are the happiness.

121
00:11:12,028 --> 00:11:12,548
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah.

122
00:11:14,086 --> 00:11:15,627
[SPEAKER_00]: You didn't want to accept and it was okay.

123
00:11:15,647 --> 00:11:16,327
[SPEAKER_00]: So, be a friend.

124
00:11:17,128 --> 00:11:17,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I have to go.

125
00:11:17,668 --> 00:11:18,188
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you go.

126
00:11:19,149 --> 00:11:22,311
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not going to justify or try to convince you.

127
00:11:22,831 --> 00:11:28,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Although, you know, the Buddha knows he's right and he knows why this guy is wrong, but he was obviously not willing to listen.

128
00:11:28,494 --> 00:11:29,314
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's another thing.

129
00:11:29,434 --> 00:11:36,258
[SPEAKER_01]: If you stop acting out of your passion, out of that sort of conviction or need to convince others,

130
00:11:37,058 --> 00:11:42,963
[SPEAKER_01]: You will also get to see who could actually hear what you have to say or who wouldn't be having the right attitude right from the start.

131
00:11:43,304 --> 00:11:48,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's kind of obvious from the beginning, but people often don't see it because they're already passionately engaged with their view.

132
00:11:50,230 --> 00:11:54,013
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't actually have the perspective to see who would hear you, who wouldn't.

133
00:11:54,493 --> 00:11:59,758
[SPEAKER_01]: So then you're just flogging a dead horse out of your own craving and need to be right or something like that.

134
00:12:01,797 --> 00:12:05,119
[SPEAKER_01]: But I think that's, in the nutshell, that's the nature of the whole, like, online forums.

135
00:12:05,159 --> 00:12:07,640
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what... it always revolves around that.

136
00:12:09,121 --> 00:12:19,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Because most often, see, it's not even... even from the start, it's not... the platform is always at a disadvantage for learning.

137
00:12:20,007 --> 00:12:23,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Because everybody comes there thinking, oh, we're all contributing to the same... No.

138
00:12:23,849 --> 00:12:25,549
[SPEAKER_01]: You either know or you don't know.

139
00:12:26,070 --> 00:12:28,311
[SPEAKER_01]: If you know, you don't need other contribution.

140
00:12:29,665 --> 00:12:32,926
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't know, nothing you contribute is of value, until you know.

141
00:12:33,826 --> 00:12:36,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like blind leading the blind arguing with the blind.

142
00:12:37,326 --> 00:12:42,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if they were to recognize the blind, they wouldn't be on the forum trying to, like, chip in or get a bit from here.

143
00:12:42,567 --> 00:12:45,768
[SPEAKER_01]: They would be somewhere else learning that which they don't know.

144
00:12:46,648 --> 00:12:57,990
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might stumble upon useful information in the forums, but don't expect great sort of learning insights, because if they happen, it will happen on your own effort and, again, not because somebody else who doesn't know helped you.

145
00:12:58,190 --> 00:12:58,890
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

146
00:13:02,548 --> 00:13:23,279
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's that other sutta you mentioned, I can't remember the title, whereby someone was disagreeing with the Buddha, and over and over again, and he said, isn't it wonderful how, even though you've been... people disagreeing with you, you're becoming even more bright.

147
00:13:23,319 --> 00:13:24,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he was bright.

148
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:26,161
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha is becoming even brighter.

149
00:13:26,261 --> 00:13:29,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he was disrespectful, that person.

150
00:13:30,423 --> 00:13:31,484
[SPEAKER_01]: He was some Brahmin, I think.

151
00:13:32,044 --> 00:13:32,284
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

152
00:13:32,705 --> 00:13:34,346
[SPEAKER_01]: He was not approving of what the Buddha was saying.

153
00:13:34,366 --> 00:13:37,168
[SPEAKER_01]: He was disrespectful, interrupting him, like more and more.

154
00:13:37,208 --> 00:13:39,970
[SPEAKER_01]: But the more he was doing that, the brighter, the calmer the Buddha was.

155
00:13:40,050 --> 00:13:44,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereas other ascetics and Brahmins, who would be... Well, that was one of the...

156
00:13:44,674 --> 00:13:52,660
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's when... when the... that's like one of the criteria a layperson should use to see whether teaching is worth being followed or not.

157
00:13:52,680 --> 00:13:57,384
[SPEAKER_01]: You find a teacher of that teaching, and when that teacher is cross-examined and questioned,

158
00:13:57,964 --> 00:13:58,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Or disagreed with.

159
00:13:59,465 --> 00:14:01,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, is he getting angry and wound up?

160
00:14:01,447 --> 00:14:04,930
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, you should not basically practice what he preaches.

161
00:14:05,431 --> 00:14:05,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

162
00:14:05,851 --> 00:14:07,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Because passion, aversion, delusion.

163
00:14:09,074 --> 00:14:09,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing he preaches.

164
00:14:09,675 --> 00:14:13,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how lofty, how great, how well taught his ideas are.

165
00:14:14,239 --> 00:14:16,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, he's passionate on account of it.

166
00:14:16,541 --> 00:14:18,763
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing worthy there of following.

167
00:14:20,244 --> 00:14:22,226
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if if I come and question you now,

168
00:14:22,940 --> 00:14:24,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even disrespectfully.

169
00:14:25,105 --> 00:14:29,956
[SPEAKER_01]: If you get angry on account of the ideas I'm presenting to you, like, you should be worried.

170
00:14:30,810 --> 00:14:35,112
[SPEAKER_01]: If you just get annoyed with my rude behavior, that's fine, but that wouldn't give rise to anger.

171
00:14:35,672 --> 00:14:39,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Or like, like you really... oh, you have to stop talking to me or something, because you're furious now.

172
00:14:40,175 --> 00:14:46,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what the Buddha would often quote in those, like, other ascetics, the Brahmins would get angry, would get like, how dare you question me?

173
00:14:47,378 --> 00:14:48,799
[SPEAKER_01]: How dare you say that to me?

174
00:14:49,379 --> 00:14:50,500
[SPEAKER_01]: So you want to see that for yourself.

175
00:14:50,560 --> 00:14:52,961
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you don't need to go and start arguing with people.

176
00:14:53,561 --> 00:14:58,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, as I said, when you read other things, you're going to come across things that that are opposing your view.

177
00:14:59,702 --> 00:15:10,345
[SPEAKER_01]: So, internally, you want to see, is there still need in me, for me, to defend my view or to prove that this is wrong because I feel challenged simply by the presence of opposing view?

178
00:15:12,165 --> 00:15:14,146
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, you are not free from doubt.

179
00:15:15,847 --> 00:15:20,988
[SPEAKER_01]: And, by the way, this applies to any worldly view, any worldly standpoint, regardless of what it is.

180
00:15:21,548 --> 00:15:25,549
[SPEAKER_01]: But when it comes to dhamma now, it's even more relevant that you are not free from doubt then.

181
00:15:25,789 --> 00:15:26,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Means it's not the dhamma.

182
00:15:27,189 --> 00:15:33,811
[SPEAKER_01]: But when it comes down to dhamma, you cannot have a valid dhamma view and not being free from doubt in regard to it at the same time.

183
00:15:34,431 --> 00:15:35,331
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the whole point.

184
00:15:35,351 --> 00:15:40,752
[SPEAKER_01]: When a sotāpanna becomes sotāpanna, when he understands that he understands, whole world thinks one way,

185
00:15:43,525 --> 00:15:48,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Against what the Buddha said, you would know that the Buddha was right, because that's what the dhamma is, which you've seen for yourself.

186
00:15:49,626 --> 00:16:06,910
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if the whole world says, no, that's not the dukkha or something, but if you yourself are free from the dukkha, free from doubt in regard to dukkha, in that universal recognition of the certain, basically, characteristics of the experience, it won't waver you, it will not perturb you, it will not disturb you.

187
00:16:07,230 --> 00:16:08,850
[SPEAKER_01]: You would just say, oh, all these people are wrong.

188
00:16:09,857 --> 00:16:10,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, I am right.

189
00:16:11,098 --> 00:16:11,859
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma is right.

190
00:16:11,919 --> 00:16:12,820
[SPEAKER_01]: The principle is right.

191
00:16:13,281 --> 00:16:14,843
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm partaking it because I understood it.

192
00:16:15,564 --> 00:16:17,506
[SPEAKER_01]: But, like, it's nothing to do with you.

193
00:16:17,526 --> 00:16:20,509
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would know that, which is why you cannot be conceited on account of it.

194
00:16:21,831 --> 00:16:23,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is not... Or feel challenged.

195
00:16:23,413 --> 00:16:24,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Or feel challenged, exactly.

196
00:16:24,534 --> 00:16:25,135
[SPEAKER_01]: It's me.

197
00:16:25,255 --> 00:16:26,777
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're self-centered, you're challenged.

198
00:16:26,837 --> 00:16:27,798
[SPEAKER_01]: When you need to defend it.

199
00:16:28,831 --> 00:16:29,472
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what everybody said.

200
00:16:29,492 --> 00:16:42,064
[SPEAKER_01]: The one who understood the dhamma, when they teach, they would, in the suttas, like, they would, they would basically, they would not say, I know, I see, in that way, but it was like, oh, this is how I understood, or this is how it has been seen.

201
00:16:42,925 --> 00:16:46,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's no need, even if it's factual, yeah, you did so for yourself.

202
00:16:47,417 --> 00:16:55,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Other people start with their conceit, which means if you approach from that point of view, even through a habit, not necessarily being conceited, they will take it wrongly.

203
00:16:55,443 --> 00:16:57,365
[SPEAKER_01]: So they won't actually hear the dhamma, although they could have heard.

204
00:16:57,965 --> 00:17:05,952
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you bypass yourself and just present it in neutral terms, the way it has been understood by you or whatever else, if they get to see the idea, they'll see you're right.

205
00:17:07,148 --> 00:17:07,648
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's what I mean.

206
00:17:07,788 --> 00:17:12,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Internally, you can already see that for yourself, on a daily basis, when your views are challenged.

207
00:17:13,491 --> 00:17:15,671
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the response in regard to it, internally?

208
00:17:15,731 --> 00:17:20,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there, like, a rising passion or fear of the challenge, because I might be wrong?

209
00:17:20,773 --> 00:17:25,735
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that just outlines you what the work still is, and you have to do it.

210
00:17:27,136 --> 00:17:29,517
[SPEAKER_00]: So, with mundane views, for example,

211
00:17:31,322 --> 00:17:34,063
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, political views... Well, same.

212
00:17:34,243 --> 00:17:34,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

213
00:17:35,044 --> 00:17:36,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Abortion, yes.

214
00:17:36,364 --> 00:17:37,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Abortion, no.

215
00:17:38,925 --> 00:17:39,826
[SPEAKER_00]: You're getting angry.

216
00:17:40,006 --> 00:17:44,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you're getting angry, it means, like, oh, so you're blindly... you're passionately defending it.

217
00:17:44,768 --> 00:17:50,170
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have fully understood, even a worldly view, that is free from contradiction,

218
00:17:51,090 --> 00:18:03,157
[SPEAKER_01]: If you clearly rationalized, like you understood it on rational terms, not emotional terms, because it pleases you, or because it's free of contradiction, then you would not be disturbed, even if people disagree.

219
00:18:04,358 --> 00:18:12,222
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you are defending or getting angry on account of your view, that you took it emotionally, not rationally, you're not free from doubt.

220
00:18:14,820 --> 00:18:25,106
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even, yeah, worldly views on some, like, you know, on some contentious issues, if you are getting wound up, means you are not clear about those issues yourself.

221
00:18:25,967 --> 00:18:29,990
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're just not overly compensating with your emotional response of defense.

222
00:18:30,030 --> 00:18:32,011
[SPEAKER_00]: You start... it's built on an emotional base.

223
00:18:32,271 --> 00:18:34,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Every view will start on the emotional base, exactly.

224
00:18:34,452 --> 00:18:38,515
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you leave it there, that's when you'll be, well, irrational.

225
00:18:39,933 --> 00:18:40,513
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the same.

226
00:18:40,633 --> 00:18:45,657
[SPEAKER_01]: It's irrational to get angry on account of ideas of dhamma, because dhamma and anger are incompatible.

227
00:18:46,377 --> 00:18:52,741
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the fact that you're getting angry because somebody's disagreeing with your views on dhamma or whatever else, means you're irrational, which means that's not the dhamma.

228
00:18:55,103 --> 00:19:08,732
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether it's a political view or whatever, view on abortion or other cultural aspects, if you're getting emotional on account of witnessing opposing views, means you are not clear about those views yourself.

229
00:19:10,251 --> 00:19:11,833
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that should be instead of...

230
00:19:11,893 --> 00:19:13,915
[SPEAKER_01]: I must conquer all these opposing views.

231
00:19:13,955 --> 00:19:14,075
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

232
00:19:14,215 --> 00:19:16,037
[SPEAKER_01]: I must actually re-evaluate my own views.

233
00:19:16,597 --> 00:19:23,504
[SPEAKER_01]: And if I rationally arrive at the same conclusion that I've sort of gratuitously took through my emotion, oh, that's great.

234
00:19:23,664 --> 00:19:33,915
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you will be... you will not be so easily moved when those views are challenged, because you have clearly understood them, or certainly have greater clarity in regard to them.

235
00:19:35,027 --> 00:19:39,370
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what is right, and it's got nothing to do with you, how you feel about it.

236
00:19:39,670 --> 00:19:40,311
[SPEAKER_00]: It is the way it is.

237
00:19:41,292 --> 00:19:42,593
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, anyone challenging you...

238
00:19:43,400 --> 00:19:47,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's just basic rational thought, that's free of contradiction, is your criteria.

239
00:19:47,383 --> 00:19:54,187
[SPEAKER_01]: And you use that, and you come to a conclusion, and then you question that conclusion for yourself, and see if it's contradiction-proof.

240
00:19:55,027 --> 00:19:56,688
[SPEAKER_01]: And if so, okay.

241
00:19:57,129 --> 00:20:06,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if somebody presents you with a new angle, that made you think, instead of shutting down that new angle, because you don't, you think further.

242
00:20:06,234 --> 00:20:07,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, thank you very much.

243
00:20:07,215 --> 00:20:08,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me now contemplate further.

244
00:20:08,276 --> 00:20:09,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't see that person as

245
00:20:10,731 --> 00:20:15,033
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not necessarily contradicting your... you, but this idea...

246
00:20:15,053 --> 00:20:16,013
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea, yeah.

247
00:20:16,093 --> 00:20:20,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if that person is attacking you, you don't need to take it personally.

248
00:20:20,355 --> 00:20:21,136
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole point.

249
00:20:21,836 --> 00:20:25,457
[SPEAKER_01]: You look at the idea they're taking, it's like, oh yeah, they're ignorant in their personal attack.

250
00:20:26,478 --> 00:20:30,680
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point they're presenting, actually, I haven't addressed in my own thinking, so I should.

251
00:20:31,598 --> 00:20:34,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Not for the sake of shutting them up or having a good comeback.

252
00:20:34,639 --> 00:20:34,779
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

253
00:20:34,819 --> 00:20:41,962
[SPEAKER_01]: For the sake of self-clarity, based on ideas and values in regard to culture and experience you are, it's quite important.

254
00:20:42,803 --> 00:20:48,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And usually, those people who do that, were people who qualify as wise men of old.

255
00:20:49,915 --> 00:20:59,478
[SPEAKER_01]: People who would not necessarily be practicing the dhamma, but they still had wisdom in regard to life, in regard to, basically, things people go through, regardless of the culture, regardless of the period.

256
00:20:59,938 --> 00:21:11,461
[SPEAKER_01]: It's people, actually, who invest their time in their own thinking, not just because, oh, this is my view and I will defend it blindly, like my religion or my identity or my nationality, but because, no, I reasoned it and this is the point.

257
00:21:12,641 --> 00:21:13,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Free from contradiction.

258
00:21:13,462 --> 00:21:17,883
[SPEAKER_01]: If you present to me something that I haven't addressed, haven't thought of, that's even better.

259
00:21:18,825 --> 00:21:20,266
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense...

260
00:21:20,286 --> 00:21:24,410
[SPEAKER_01]: So, with the right attitude, discussion, you can then learn from it.

261
00:21:25,371 --> 00:21:30,615
[SPEAKER_01]: But most people engage in discussion to either convince or kind of self-defend.

262
00:21:32,677 --> 00:21:38,001
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, by convincing you to agree with me, I kind of get validation of my own views.

263
00:21:38,041 --> 00:21:39,242
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, just ask yourself.

264
00:21:40,483 --> 00:21:41,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Are those views free from doubt?

265
00:21:43,566 --> 00:21:46,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Are those views... Do I feel a need to defend them?

266
00:21:47,808 --> 00:21:51,451
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, means I am emotionally bound with those views, which means I am irrational.

267
00:21:51,551 --> 00:21:55,695
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to get... if you want to be right, or do you want the right idea?

268
00:21:56,836 --> 00:21:57,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

269
00:21:57,316 --> 00:22:00,899
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if you... if you actually invest the effort into right idea, that will make you right.

270
00:22:01,680 --> 00:22:05,283
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would not... you wouldn't feel conceited on account of being right.

271
00:22:05,955 --> 00:22:07,576
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the best kind of discussion.

272
00:22:07,676 --> 00:22:10,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Two people are interested in the right idea.

273
00:22:10,658 --> 00:22:10,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

274
00:22:11,259 --> 00:22:15,101
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even though they don't have the right idea, still, that is... Well, they meet halfway.

275
00:22:15,262 --> 00:22:16,462
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a kind of... Yeah, exactly.

276
00:22:17,003 --> 00:22:23,848
[SPEAKER_01]: So, both of them are willing to step outside, or at least step away, from that emotional basis of their view.

277
00:22:24,528 --> 00:22:26,430
[SPEAKER_01]: And discuss terms on the level of idea.

278
00:22:27,091 --> 00:22:32,696
[SPEAKER_01]: And that space that you create by stepping out, just tiny bit, might be enough, actually, to re-evaluate.

279
00:22:33,096 --> 00:22:38,742
[SPEAKER_01]: And... because emotional... emotions are not needed to hold the view at all.

280
00:22:39,262 --> 00:22:42,205
[SPEAKER_01]: They obscure even the right view, if you were to hold it rightly.

281
00:22:42,866 --> 00:22:44,988
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't be right, because you're emotional in regard to it.

282
00:22:46,549 --> 00:22:49,032
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't want to be emotional about any of the views.

283
00:22:51,232 --> 00:22:53,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's worldly or on the level of the dhamma.

284
00:22:53,193 --> 00:23:00,596
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you are, means you need more clarity whereby, if you develop it, there is no room for emotion to arise.

285
00:23:01,136 --> 00:23:05,458
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody agrees with you, you will not be over the moon or feel validated.

286
00:23:05,558 --> 00:23:08,959
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody disagrees with you, you will not be upset or angry with them.

287
00:23:10,460 --> 00:23:11,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can separate...

288
00:23:11,760 --> 00:23:13,321
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if somebody misrepresents you.

289
00:23:14,604 --> 00:23:15,504
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be upset with it.

290
00:23:15,524 --> 00:23:21,106
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll try and correct it, but if they refuse and they keep basically like saying, like, that you said that, which you didn't.

291
00:23:21,866 --> 00:23:23,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you know, he meant this, but no, you didn't.

292
00:23:23,526 --> 00:23:27,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Like when in the suttas, ah, the Buddha meant sensuality is not an obstacle.

293
00:23:27,927 --> 00:23:29,728
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, oh, don't represent the Tathāgata.

294
00:23:29,828 --> 00:23:30,968
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a very bad kamma.

295
00:23:31,768 --> 00:23:32,708
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no, not really.

296
00:23:32,748 --> 00:23:33,528
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what he said.

297
00:23:33,548 --> 00:23:34,489
[SPEAKER_01]: This is how I understood it.

298
00:23:36,229 --> 00:23:39,330
[SPEAKER_01]: And on that occasion, the Buddha was able to correct him, but I'm sure there were many

299
00:23:44,978 --> 00:23:45,498
[SPEAKER_01]: And just leave.

300
00:23:45,518 --> 00:23:46,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Not talk to them.

301
00:23:46,279 --> 00:23:46,659
[SPEAKER_01]: They're done.

302
00:23:48,600 --> 00:23:50,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's nothing you would say that would change that.

303
00:23:50,281 --> 00:23:54,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you say would just add to that misrepresentation already.

304
00:23:54,644 --> 00:24:00,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's often something where people can feel wound up while being misrepresented.

305
00:24:00,608 --> 00:24:01,609
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't really say that.

306
00:24:01,629 --> 00:24:04,070
[SPEAKER_01]: It was like, yeah, that's where the world is.

307
00:24:04,090 --> 00:24:06,372
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just blind leading the blind in every regard, so...

308
00:24:07,061 --> 00:24:10,022
[SPEAKER_01]: You shouldn't expect to be represented correctly.

309
00:24:10,242 --> 00:24:19,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means if you... whether you are represented correctly or misrepresented, if you're emotionally disturbed on account of it, you are in the wrong for that reason.

310
00:24:19,747 --> 00:24:28,931
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if the whole world is misrepresenting you, the whole world is against you, the whole world thinks your views are wrong, you should not be emotionally perturbed on account of that.

311
00:24:29,931 --> 00:24:33,313
[SPEAKER_01]: And if your view is right, you will not be emotionally perturbed on account of that.

312
00:24:39,312 --> 00:24:43,315
[SPEAKER_01]: And, if you develop that, then you are truly in a position to help others.

313
00:24:43,375 --> 00:24:46,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing short of that.

314
00:24:46,477 --> 00:24:51,520
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a doctor who gives people medicine to people just so they will agree with him, just so they will praise him.

315
00:24:52,701 --> 00:24:55,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Medicine might work or not, but it's not up to his knowledge, isn't it?

316
00:24:55,622 --> 00:24:56,303
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a chance.

317
00:24:58,709 --> 00:25:08,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Or a doctor who truly knows what the cause is, what the cure is, he wouldn't care if the patient says, oh, this medicine is bitter and I hate you and so on.

318
00:25:09,016 --> 00:25:11,018
[SPEAKER_01]: He still knows that's the factual medicine.

319
00:25:11,698 --> 00:25:15,721
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that person refuses to take it, he won't lose any sleep.

320
00:25:15,841 --> 00:25:16,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would he?

321
00:25:17,502 --> 00:25:24,947
[SPEAKER_01]: But if he was doing it for the emotional validation, for his great ego, because he's the greatest doctor, means you're a bad doctor for that very reason.

322
00:25:27,377 --> 00:25:28,718
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not seeing problem clearly.

323
00:25:29,258 --> 00:25:30,339
[SPEAKER_01]: You're taking it personally.

324
00:25:31,300 --> 00:25:33,681
[SPEAKER_01]: And actually, I think, even in the medical field, that's the whole point.

325
00:25:34,002 --> 00:25:37,684
[SPEAKER_01]: If a doctor gets personally involved, he must be excused from the case.

326
00:25:38,465 --> 00:25:41,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Perspective goes, always, even in mundane terms, when that happens.

327
00:25:42,267 --> 00:25:52,875
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why I was saying that in slightly different context, but that's why there is no... if you do... if you start doing things on this level, there is no difference between, like, a study of the dhamma and the

328
00:25:54,516 --> 00:25:56,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Formal, like, practice and application of it.

329
00:25:57,017 --> 00:25:58,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Study becomes the practice.

330
00:25:59,959 --> 00:26:09,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Practice becomes the further study.

331
00:26:09,144 --> 00:26:10,705
[SPEAKER_00]: The application.

332
00:26:10,765 --> 00:26:12,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I studied, now I have to go and apply.

333
00:26:12,666 --> 00:26:14,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Your study should be the application.

334
00:26:14,627 --> 00:26:15,668
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you measure application?

335
00:26:16,148 --> 00:26:20,731
[SPEAKER_01]: By basically dissipating of greed, aversion, delusion and non-ag... non-reason, greed, aversion, delusion.

336
00:26:21,400 --> 00:26:26,503
[SPEAKER_01]: It's less likely to arise if you engage in these ideas and these thoughts and maintain them in your mind.

337
00:26:26,583 --> 00:26:27,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Cultivate them.

338
00:26:27,284 --> 00:26:27,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Bhāvanā.

339
00:26:30,105 --> 00:26:34,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Cultivate certain perceptions, you will become impervious to lust.

340
00:26:36,149 --> 00:26:36,849
[SPEAKER_00]: And how do you cultivate?

341
00:26:36,869 --> 00:26:42,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, just think about, yes, this is what a Subha is, and this is what Agni is, and this is the last of this... No, no.

342
00:26:42,693 --> 00:26:49,457
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to cultivate it on the level whereby it applies to you on that practical level, so there is no lust in you, and there cannot be lust.

343
00:26:50,084 --> 00:26:57,166
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you start, you will have to, as I said, inevitably start on the level of idea, but then you want to see, is this idea freeing me?

344
00:26:57,206 --> 00:26:57,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

345
00:26:58,506 --> 00:26:59,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's the thing.

346
00:26:59,467 --> 00:27:05,728
[SPEAKER_01]: First you start, but then, yes, you can, instead of, oh, let me, let me kind of start on this idea that I think I should start.

347
00:27:05,768 --> 00:27:05,928
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

348
00:27:06,008 --> 00:27:08,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Just re-discern the ideas already there.

349
00:27:08,889 --> 00:27:11,390
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already the result of doing things rightly.

350
00:27:11,410 --> 00:27:19,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of me trying to kind of build up these ideas so I can contemplate, instead, I just recognize what's already there and I see those ideas already around me.

351
00:27:19,945 --> 00:27:22,570
[SPEAKER_01]: idea of ugly, idea of death, all these contemplations.

352
00:27:23,431 --> 00:27:25,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is already there.

353
00:27:25,154 --> 00:27:33,668
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of doing it under your own terms of thinking, you're just doing under the terms that it's already presenting itself, but you're just ignoring it in the past.

354
00:27:36,636 --> 00:27:41,000
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean, like, even if you practice mindfulness of the body, even that's an idea in people's mind.

355
00:27:41,621 --> 00:27:48,527
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can, okay, mindfulness of the body and having, like, an image of your body and now you... and the moment you lose that image, you lost your mindfulness of the body.

356
00:27:48,548 --> 00:27:53,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Or instead, oh, mindfulness of the body is the idea, but instead of giving in to pursuing that idea,

357
00:27:54,958 --> 00:27:58,480
[SPEAKER_01]: You go back to where the body is and re-discern the ideas already there.

358
00:27:59,181 --> 00:28:07,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whether you think about mindfulness of the body or not, look, the body is seated throughout all that time, hands are moving, I'm speaking, all of this is already enduring.

359
00:28:08,486 --> 00:28:13,589
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what I meant when I said in the past, you find your body already doing things there, even when you don't intend.

360
00:28:14,369 --> 00:28:16,631
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it doesn't require your intention to do it.

361
00:28:18,347 --> 00:28:19,188
[SPEAKER_01]: In that direct sense.

362
00:28:20,008 --> 00:28:27,512
[SPEAKER_01]: But usually, what people see, it's only that kind of body that appears after you intended to do things with the body.

363
00:28:28,633 --> 00:28:37,998
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why most people's meditation becomes that overly controlling kind of aspect of, I'm sitting, I'm sitting, I'm sitting, I'm intending to sit, I'm moving, I'm moving.

364
00:28:38,058 --> 00:28:42,481
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't sustain that, because that's actually secondary to the already living body that does these things.

365
00:28:43,380 --> 00:28:59,527
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of you fabricating these contemplations and ideas, the way you think you should be practicing, you need to kind of refrain from that fabrication, and then see the body already doing that, and then discern sitting, walking, talking, as already there, done by the body in itself.

366
00:29:00,387 --> 00:29:12,452
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha said, when a monk establishes his mind in first jhāna, whether he sits, walks, extends his arm, goes to the toilet, whatever he does, is done with full mindfulness, and it's divine in that state.

367
00:29:13,782 --> 00:29:16,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he's not doing it, he's just seeing it being done.

368
00:29:17,226 --> 00:29:19,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind has withdrawn from greed, aversion, delusion.

369
00:29:20,671 --> 00:29:21,412
[SPEAKER_00]: You're effortless.

370
00:29:21,672 --> 00:29:29,562
[SPEAKER_01]: So, same when you're thinking, oh, I need to... you know, there's this sutra, right, that made me think... You're going to go into that abstract direction, but instead of that...

371
00:29:30,461 --> 00:29:33,682
[SPEAKER_01]: You sort of refrain and then find those ideas is already there.

372
00:29:33,702 --> 00:29:36,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Enduring on the level of present, enduring body, experience as a whole.

373
00:29:37,144 --> 00:29:41,686
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you try to find those those phenomena that in the suttas...

374
00:29:41,706 --> 00:29:55,972
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether it's a subha, whether it's contemplation of death, whether it's body postures, whether it's thinking about base of nothingness, doesn't matter how refined, how it is, you want to find it as it is.

375
00:29:56,472 --> 00:29:58,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Present experience, enduring,

376
00:29:59,057 --> 00:30:09,640
[SPEAKER_01]: On that practical level, as I say, not on the level of these ideas which you might have to engage, depending on, you know, what your kind of certain inclinations are.

377
00:30:10,280 --> 00:30:15,101
[SPEAKER_01]: But if that's all you engage with, you're not anything closer to understanding.

378
00:30:15,121 --> 00:30:17,001
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, it could be worse as well.

379
00:30:17,281 --> 00:30:22,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you think those ideas are the dhamma, you will then not be seeking them on the practical level.

380
00:30:22,623 --> 00:30:27,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and then expect some practical result on account of you keeping those ideas in your mind.

381
00:30:27,903 --> 00:30:30,845
[SPEAKER_01]: No, the only practical result is when you practically start seeing them.

382
00:30:36,489 --> 00:30:37,490
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the cultivation.

383
00:30:37,710 --> 00:30:38,891
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly it, Bhāvananda.

384
00:30:38,911 --> 00:30:41,473
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintaining it in the being, presently enduring it.

385
00:30:42,734 --> 00:30:43,254
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it is.

386
00:30:43,274 --> 00:30:44,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Discerning it is already there.

387
00:30:45,356 --> 00:30:49,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Not on your own terms, the way you got these ideas and abstractions, but on the terms it's,

388
00:30:51,360 --> 00:30:53,622
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't feel right when I think of the seated body.

389
00:30:53,642 --> 00:30:54,764
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how it feels.

390
00:30:55,324 --> 00:30:56,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Is the body seated?

391
00:30:56,525 --> 00:30:56,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

392
00:30:57,046 --> 00:30:57,406
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

393
00:30:57,526 --> 00:30:58,167
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your knowledge.

394
00:30:58,708 --> 00:31:02,171
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to think of... or let me now practice seated meditation.

395
00:31:02,952 --> 00:31:04,954
[SPEAKER_01]: You are already seated.

396
00:31:05,014 --> 00:31:06,275
[SPEAKER_01]: The phenomena is already enduring.

397
00:31:06,696 --> 00:31:08,398
[SPEAKER_01]: The body is already doing the sitting.

398
00:31:09,519 --> 00:31:10,920
[SPEAKER_01]: So don't lose the sight of that.

399
00:31:11,040 --> 00:31:13,663
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your meditation, regardless of how you feel about it.

400
00:31:15,727 --> 00:31:32,977
[SPEAKER_01]: And then all that, that sight of body sitting there, implies all the other phenomena that... Well, everything else will come from it, but I don't want to give too much information, because people end up having ideas about it, instead of... No, okay, am I seated while I'm listening to this talk?

401
00:31:33,057 --> 00:31:34,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I already know that.

402
00:31:35,959 --> 00:31:40,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I need to now, oh, I'm sitting, I'm sitting, I'm sitting, I'm sitting, or I'll think about... No.

403
00:31:41,623 --> 00:31:45,285
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already there, so all I need to do is not completely lose the sight of it.

404
00:31:46,487 --> 00:31:52,030
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't feel as as active, as satisfying, as engaging, when you actively engage and try to figure things out.

405
00:31:52,911 --> 00:31:55,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it doesn't feel like that, because it doesn't have the craving.

406
00:31:56,093 --> 00:31:59,094
[SPEAKER_01]: And your starting point is the point with craving.

407
00:31:59,114 --> 00:32:00,675
[SPEAKER_01]: So craving will feel more normal.

408
00:32:01,376 --> 00:32:03,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving will feel more palpable.

409
00:32:03,997 --> 00:32:06,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking with craving, meditating with craving, and so on.

410
00:32:06,819 --> 00:32:08,160
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I am doing this through my

411
00:32:09,160 --> 00:32:10,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Craved determination.

412
00:32:11,181 --> 00:32:24,329
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you stop using how you feel about it as a criteria, but instead, no, the idea of seated body is still there, enduring, and I don't need to maintain it, and it displeases me, because I'm not doing it on my own terms, that's fine.

413
00:32:25,050 --> 00:32:25,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Great.

414
00:32:25,370 --> 00:32:29,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I have this pleasure to be mindful of as enduring there, not as my own doing.

415
00:32:31,573 --> 00:32:36,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, whether it's pleasant feeling, unpleasant feeling, you will be seeing it for what it is, enduring there, in your mind.

416
00:32:37,813 --> 00:32:41,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Not being pulled by it, not trying to get rid of it or prolong it.

417
00:32:43,876 --> 00:32:48,438
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's also when it's going to become apparent that most people's meditation revolves simply around how they feel.

418
00:32:49,479 --> 00:32:51,740
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you felt good, meditation was good.

419
00:32:52,661 --> 00:32:54,882
[SPEAKER_01]: If you didn't feel good, meditation wasn't good.

420
00:32:55,862 --> 00:33:00,605
[SPEAKER_01]: But feeling should not be the criteria for anything, least of all for meditation.

421
00:33:01,965 --> 00:33:02,866
[SPEAKER_00]: The discernment.

422
00:33:03,362 --> 00:33:04,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

423
00:33:04,203 --> 00:33:05,004
[SPEAKER_00]: The clarity.

424
00:33:05,765 --> 00:33:10,932
[SPEAKER_01]: The clarity in regard to the enduring idea, the clarity in regard to the enduring experience and the phenomena.

425
00:33:13,355 --> 00:33:17,179
[SPEAKER_01]: The clarity in regard to whatever emotional current pressure you might be feeling.

